INSERT INTO public.reading_sets (
    set_id, title, title_zh, grade, difficulty, topic, passage,
    word_count, estimated_minutes, sort_order, version, updated_at
) VALUES (
    'r08-04',
    $seedie$Shared Bikes in Our City$seedie$,
    $seedie$我们城市里的共享单车$seedie$,
    8,
    $seedie$medium$seedie$,
    $seedie$public_life$seedie$,
    $seedie$Shared bikes have become a common part of city life. With a smartphone, people can unlock a bike, ride to the subway station or school, and leave it in a parking area. For many students, shared bikes save time on short trips. They are also cheaper than taxis for everyday travel.

However, shared bikes also bring problems. Some riders leave bikes in the middle of sidewalks. Then parents with strollers and older people with walking sticks find it hard to pass. A few people damage QR codes or throw bikes into rivers. These actions make the service worse for everyone and create extra work for city workers.

To improve the situation, our city made clearer rules last year. Riders must park inside white lines near bus stops and schools. If a bike is left in a wrong place too often by the same account, the app may add a small fine. Companies also hire workers to move bikes at busy hours. Some schools now teach "bike manners" in moral education class. Students learn that public tools need public care.

Shared bikes are not just machines. They are a test of how we share space in a busy city. When we park carefully, we protect other people's safety. When we report broken bikes in the app, we help the next rider. Technology can make travel easier, but good habits make the city kinder. Next time you finish a ride, take ten more seconds to park well. That small action matters.$seedie$,
    262,
    8,
    3,
    1,
    2026072901
)
