INSERT INTO public.reading_sets (
    set_id, title, title_zh, grade, difficulty, topic, passage,
    word_count, estimated_minutes, sort_order, version, updated_at
) VALUES (
    'r08-05',
    $seedie$A Morning at the Science Museum$seedie$,
    $seedie$科学博物馆的一上午$seedie$,
    8,
    $seedie$medium$seedie$,
    $seedie$stem_visit$seedie$,
    $seedie$Last Saturday, our class visited the city science museum. Many students expected only quiet halls and glass boxes. Instead, the museum felt alive. In the first hall, we tried a <<hands-on>> weather station. By turning a handle, we could make wind stronger and watch how paper clouds moved. A guide explained that scientists use real tools to measure wind speed and rainfall.

Next, we entered the energy room. There, a bicycle was connected to a small light. When a classmate pedaled faster, the light became brighter. When she slowed down, the light almost went out. We laughed, but we also understood a serious idea: energy does not appear from nowhere. If we want light, heat, or motion, something must provide power.

The most surprising part was the robotics corner. Two student volunteers from a high school showed a robot that could pick up plastic bottles and drop them into a recycling box. The robot was slow and sometimes missed the bottle. Still, the volunteers said failure was part of building. They changed the program twice while we watched.

Before we left, each group chose one exhibit and wrote three questions about it. My group asked how the robot "sees" objects, who repairs museum machines, and whether teenagers can join weekend workshops. The guide gave us a leaflet with club information. On the bus back to school, the noise was different. Students were not only talking about funny moments. They were also arguing about ideas. That morning did not make us scientists at once, but it made science feel close enough to touch.$seedie$,
    268,
    8,
    4,
    1,
    2026072901
)
